Description: compiler for the IMP72 systems programming language
 IMP72 is the extensible systems language of Ned Irons (IMP stands for Irons
 Meta Processor). This package is a
 compiler for it on 64-bit machines, derived from the IMP72 compiler Walter
 Bilofsky wrote for the PDP-10 (DECUS 10-198a, 1973).
 .
 The language is defined by a file of syntax statements that the compiler
 reads; extensions are written in the same notation. imp compiles programs
 to object files, executables, assembly or LLVM IR, and links executables with
 lld. LLVM and lld run inside the compiler, so no C compiler is needed at run
 time; the C library's development files provide the start files and the
 library to link with.
 .
 A subroutine is a C function and takes its arguments by reference, as in
 FORTRAN. CCALL calls a C function with arguments by value, and an object file
 made by imp can be linked with C.
 .
 The package also installs impstage0, the bootstrap compiler that builds
 imp from source, and the manual.
